  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B026",
        "laureates": [
          "Mehran Kardar",
          "Yi-Cheng Zhang",
          "Kazumasa A. Takeuchi"
        ],
        "citation": "For establishing the theory of universal fluctuations in nonequilibrium interface growth and demonstrating their universality experimentally."
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B052",
    "B003"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "I retain B026 as my preferred configuration. KPZ gives a compact theoretical account of interface-growth fluctuations, and testing its predictions in multiple experimental systems supports both its maturity and its reach across statistical physics. That combination of a general mechanism and experimental confirmation is the central merit of the case.",
    "case_against": "The proposed three-name credit still needs stronger historical support. The packet does not establish how the theoretical contribution is divided among the shortlisted names or how each relates to the experimental confirmation. The citation should not imply equal contributions unless the historical record supports that account.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "danielsson-ulf",
        "point": "Danielsson favors WMAP for turning cosmology into a quantitative science, while noting the challenge of assigning individual credit within a large mission and distinguishing it from other CMB work.",
        "response": "WMAP's scientific maturity and lasting quantitative impact are strong. My preference remains with KPZ because the proposed achievement can be stated as a general framework tested across multiple systems, while the WMAP account must also resolve individual credit within a large mission and its boundary with other CMB measurements. That comparison concerns scope and attribution, not a ranking or support count."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson favors circuit QED for its conceptual and experimental influence, but flags its boundary with earlier superconducting-circuit work and the 2025 prize.",
        "response": "I agree that circuit QED's established platform has substantial scientific merit even if later computing applications remain unsettled. Its unresolved boundary makes the final citation harder to delimit. KPZ's defined universality class and experimental tests offer a more bounded account. I have not changed my preferred configuration, but the chair's attribution question leads me to treat the proposed division of credit among the three B026 names as a material unresolved issue."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "A historical review should establish the respective contributions of Kardar and Yi-Cheng Zhang to the theoretical framework and Takeuchi to experimental confirmation, and verify that these shortlisted names capture the decisive work."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "My profile records no specific collaboration or institutional tie to the B026 nominees. It does note that the Gothenburg and Chalmers physics departments are closely linked; Johansson and Olsson are committee colleagues in that environment. This institutional proximity should be disclosed, though no direct conflict concerning the proposed nominees is apparent."
  }
